//! English explanations of RADIUS (G4.3j), scope `radius`, sorted by key.

/// Explanations: (key, Markdown).
pub(super) const HELP: &[(&str, &str)] = &[
    ("menu.radius", "## What it does\nThe RADIUS servers the router asks for PPP, IPoE, hotspot and login (RouterOS `/radius`): failover order, live counters per server (`/radius monitor`), CoA and Disconnect from the billing system (`/radius incoming`, RFC 5176) and a Test button that sends an Access-Request for a test user and shows the reply decoded."),
    ("radius.accounting_backup", "## Accounting Backup\n\nRouterOS `accounting-backup`: this server gets accounting only after the non-backup ones (in list order). For a server that only keeps a copy.\n\nAuthentication is unchanged: list order."),
    ("radius.accounting_port", "## Accounting Port\n\nThe server's UDP port for Accounting-Request (Start, Interim-Update, Stop). Default **1813** (RFC 2866 §3). Must differ from the authentication port."),
    ("radius.address", "## Address\n\nThe RADIUS server's IP (IPv4 or IPv6). The same server may be listed more than once for different services, never twice for the same one.\n\n**List order is failover order**: the first that answers is used; a server failing 3 exchanges in a row goes *down* and the next takes over."),
    ("radius.authentication_port", "## Authentication Port\n\nThe server's UDP port for Access-Request. Default **1812** (RFC 2865 §3). Very old servers use 1645."),
    ("radius.called_id", "## Called ID\n\nThe **Called-Station-Id** sent when the caller gives none (RouterOS: the PPPoE service name, or the PPTP/L2TP server IP). Optional.\n\nThe first entry of the service gives it (attributes are the same across the failover)."),
    ("radius.disabled", "## Disabled\n\nThe entry stays in the configuration, but nothing asks it and it leaves the CoA allow-list."),
    ("radius.incoming", "## Incoming (CoA and Disconnect)\n\nRouterOS `/radius incoming`: the billing system sends **CoA** (change the rate, say) or **Disconnect** (drop the session) to the router, per RFC 5176.\n\n**Off by default**: a Disconnect can drop any subscriber. Only requests from the `/radius` servers are accepted (each checked with its secret); any other source is dropped without a reply."),
    ("radius.incoming.accept", "## Accept\n\nOpens the CoA/Disconnect port. Off closes it at once."),
    ("radius.incoming.allowed", "## Allowed Sources\n\nWho may send CoA and Disconnect: the addresses of the **enabled** `/radius` entries, each with its secret — as in RouterOS. A request from another address is dropped silently (RFC 5176 §5) and counted in *dropped*."),
    ("radius.incoming.listening", "## Listening\n\nWhere the CoA/Disconnect port is open now, and the counters: checked requests, **ACK**, **NAK** (with Error-Cause), dropped (unknown source, wrong secret, stale Event-Timestamp), duplicates (retransmissions answered from the cache) and the **session owners** (PPP and IPoE register when they exist).\n\nWithout a session owner every request gets NAK with Error-Cause **503** (Session Context Not Found)."),
    ("radius.incoming.port", "## Port\n\nThe CoA/Disconnect UDP port. The default here is **3799**, RFC 5176 §2.3's (FreeRADIUS, Juniper and Cisco use it too).\n\n**MikroTik uses 1700**: if your billing system already sends to 1700 (it talked to a RouterOS), set **1700** here. Both work; both sides must agree."),
    ("radius.incoming.replay_window", "## Replay Window\n\nHow long (s) a request's Event-Timestamp is valid, and how long a retransmission gets the cached answer without being applied again. Default **300 s** (RFC 5176 §6.3).\n\nA request with an Event-Timestamp outside the window is dropped: no recorded request replayed later. Needs both clocks right (NTP)."),
    ("radius.incoming.require_event_timestamp", "## Require Event-Timestamp\n\nDrop requests without Event-Timestamp (RFC 5176 §6.3 asks for it to be configurable). Off by default: most billing systems do not send it. Turn it on if yours does, to close replay."),
    ("radius.incoming.require_message_auth", "## Require Message-Auth\n\nDrop requests without Message-Authenticator (RFC 2869 §5.14). Off by default for the same reason as Event-Timestamp; on once every sender adds it (Blast-RADIUS, CVE-2024-3596)."),
    ("radius.incoming.vrf", "## VRF\n\nWhich VRF the CoA port listens in. `main` is the main table."),
    ("radius.monitor", "## Monitor\n\nRouterOS `/radius monitor`, live, per server (authentication and accounting summed): state, pending, requests, accepts, rejects, accounting responses, resends, timeouts, bad replies, the last request's RTT and how many sockets the client opened (256 requests in flight per socket)."),
    ("radius.monitor.accepts", "## Accepts\n\nAccess-Accept received (login accepted)."),
    ("radius.monitor.acct_responses", "## Acct\n\nAccounting-Response received: accounting the server confirmed."),
    ("radius.monitor.bad_replies", "## Bad Replies\n\nReplies dropped because they did not check: a **wrong secret** (most often), a bad Response Authenticator, or no Message-Authenticator while it is required (an old server). Rising steadily: check the secret on both sides."),
    ("radius.monitor.last_request_rtt", "## RTT\n\nRound trip of the last answered request, ms."),
    ("radius.monitor.pending", "## Pending\n\nRequests sent to this server waiting for an answer now."),
    ("radius.monitor.rejects", "## Rejects\n\nAccess-Reject received (wrong user or password, account blocked in the billing system)."),
    ("radius.monitor.requests", "## Requests\n\nRequests (authentication and accounting) sent to this server, resends not counted."),
    ("radius.monitor.resends", "## Resends\n\nRetransmissions: the server did not answer in time and the request went again, unchanged, with a growing wait (RFC 5080 §2.2.1). Many: a slow server or loss on the way."),
    ("radius.monitor.sockets", "## Sockets\n\nUDP sockets the client opened for this server. Each has 256 identifiers; under load the client opens another (up to 16, 4096 requests in flight)."),
    ("radius.monitor.timeouts", "## Timeouts\n\nExchanges where the server answered none of the tries. Three in a row put the server *down* and the next in the list takes over."),
    ("radius.ports", "## Ports\n\nAuthentication / accounting (default 1812/1813)."),
    ("radius.realm", "## Realm\n\nAppended as `@realm` to a User-Name without one (`client1` becomes `client1@isp.example`), like RouterOS `realm`. Optional; no `@` or spaces."),
    ("radius.require_message_auth", "## Require Message-Auth\n\n**yes-for-request-resp** (default, like RouterOS 7.15+): every Access-Request carries Message-Authenticator and every reply must bring a valid one — the defence against **Blast-RADIUS** (CVE-2024-3596).\n\n**no**: accept replies without it. Only for an old server that cannot sign; the requirement then drops for every server of the same service. Symptom of an old server with it required: *Bad Replies* rising in Monitor and every login failing."),
    ("radius.secret", "## Secret\n\nThe secret shared with the server (RFC 2865 §3): it signs every packet and hides the PAP password. **Required**, up to 128 bytes, the same on both sides.\n\nWrite-only: the list shows `***` and the command history masks it. When editing, leave it empty to keep it."),
    ("radius.server", "## Servers\n\nRouterOS `/radius`: the servers the router asks, **in failover order**. Each says which services it serves (ppp, login, hotspot, dhcp…).\n\n**Test…** sends an Access-Request for a test user and shows the reply decoded — the quick way to check secret, port and attributes before subscribers."),
    ("radius.service", "## Service\n\nWho uses this server, comma separated: **ppp** (PPPoE), **login** (into the router), **hotspot**, **dhcp** (IPoE), **wireless**, **ipsec**, **dot1x**. For PPP to ask RADIUS, also set `ppp.aaa use_radius`."),
    ("radius.src_address", "## Src. Address\n\nWhich of the router's addresses requests leave from. Empty: the kernel picks by route. Must be an address of this router (or loopback), of the server's family.\n\nUse it when the server checks the NAS by source IP (the NAS entry in the billing system)."),
    ("radius.state", "## State\n\n**up**: answering. **down**: failed 3 exchanges in a row; skipped for 30 s. **probing**: down and probed with Status-Server (RFC 5997) until it answers. **idle**: nothing in the router uses it yet (or no daemon). **disabled**: disabled."),
    ("radius.status_server", "## Status-Server\n\nProbe a down server with Status-Server (RFC 5997) and bring it back as soon as it answers. Off by default: old servers ignore Status-Server and would stay *down*. Without it a down server is skipped for 30 s, then tried with a real request."),
    ("radius.test", "## Test\n\nSends an **Access-Request** (PAP) for a test user through the router's RADIUS service — a subscriber login's own path: failover, retransmission, Message-Authenticator — and shows the reply: accepted or rejected, by which server, how long, and the **decoded attributes** (`Mikrotik-Rate-Limit`, `Framed-IP-Address`, `Session-Timeout`…).\n\nRouterOS has none: there you test with a real subscriber or `radtest` on another machine."),
    ("radius.test.password", "## Password\n\nThe test user's password, sent as PAP (hidden with the secret, RFC 2865 §5.2). Empty: none (authorization by MAC or circuit-id, as IPoE does)."),
    ("radius.test.user", "## User\n\nThe test's User-Name (the entry's `realm` is appended without `@`). Use a test account of the billing system, not a real subscriber."),
    ("radius.timeout", "## Timeout\n\nRouterOS `timeout`: how long the router waits for the first answer, ms (default **1100**). Then it resends with the wait doubling (1.1 s, 2.2 s, 4.4 s; RFC 5080 §2.2.1) and, still nothing, moves to the next server. Raise it for a distant server; below the real latency it only causes resends."),
    ("radius.vrf", "## VRF\n\nWhich VRF reaches the server (RouterOS `address=…@vrf`). `main` is the main table. Outside `main` the socket is bound to the VRF (`SO_BINDTODEVICE`)."),
];
